   "file": "story-013-1.opus",
   "dur": 155.642,
   "chars": 2348,
   "speech": "“About a year ago, among dreams whose sensations and details have an almost real significance for me, owing to their, so to speak, printed vividness, I caught a motif, an inexpressible melody which has pursued me since then almost every night. This melody passes all bounds of expressing its power and its qualities by the ordinary means of words; hearing it, I am ready to believe in the music of the spheres; there is something inhuman in its majesty, while the beauty of its harmonies immeasurably surpasses all that has ever yet been played by trumpets and strings. It is built according to laws unknown to us. On waking I remember nothing and, clinging in vain to the impression, the one thing left to me on these occasions, like a feather of the firebird, I try to discover the spring whose sweet drop increases tenfold the thirst of one perishing in a waterless land. Perhaps in sleep our soul is more receptive; though I know, though I remember, that I have heard this wondrous music, I am nevertheless powerless to hold in my memory even a single bar. However that may be, I earnestly beg you to apply all your art either to strengthening my memory, or else, if it is possible, to the direct power of suggestion, under whose irresistible pressure I might play, in your presence (I have brought my violin), all that so distinctly stirs me in my sleep. This experiment may yield two consequences of differing importance: the first, that the perfection of the mysterious music will prove to be a dreaming distortion of the senses, as often happens with those who dream that they are reading a book of the highest talent, and yet, on waking, recall only a string of senseless phrases; in that case, convinced of my self-deception, I shall resort to systematic treatment, quite content in the knowledge that I lose but little by it; the second consequence, the fixing of the melody in notes, is immeasurably more important. Perhaps the whole musical world of times past and present will vanish in new discoveries, as seeds vanish on becoming flowers, or as the caterpillar ceases, at the appointed hour, to be a hidden, exultant butterfly. Perhaps the very consciousness of mankind will change, shifted upon its foundations, because, I repeat it, and in this I believe myself, the power of that music has in it something divine and shattering.”",
